* START-HISTORY:
* 21 Aug 26 Windows port - THE VERB IS MODIFY.PASSWORD; IT WAS SET.PASSWORD.
*           Owner's decision, 21 Aug 2026: it is used to CHANGE a password,
*           and every account now has one from the moment it is created, so
*           "set" described a first-time act that no longer exists.  It joins MODIFY.ACCOUNT
*           under one naming.  The SOURCE FILE keeps its name, as DELACC does
*           for DELETE.ACCOUNT and CREATEA for CREATE.ACCOUNT - the file id is
*           not the verb and renaming it would churn the build lists for
*           nothing.  What moved is the $catalog name and the VOC record.
* 20 Aug 26 Windows port - refuse a trailing token instead of discarding it.
*           "MODIFY.PASSWORD DON hunter2" used to prompt anyway and say nothing,
*           so the password reached the command stack for no purpose.
* 19 Aug 26 Windows port - SDSYS accounts and $cred are lower case on disk.
*           PROJECT_STATUS.md 5.12 (a).
* 13 Aug 26 Windows port - written for the account credential model
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    MODIFY.PASSWORD [account]
*
*    Sets the password of an SD account.  With no account name, sets your own.
*
*    THE PASSWORD IS NEVER AN ARGUMENT.  It is read from two hidden prompts,
*    and anything after the account name is refused rather than ignored - see
*    the comment on that test below.
*
*    Setting your own password requires the current one.  Setting someone
*    else's requires administrator rights, and does not, since an
*    administrator resetting a forgotten password does not know it.
*
*    The account must exist in the ACCOUNTS register.  See PROJECT_STATUS.md
*    section 5.6.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
program set_acc_password
$catalog $MODIFY.PASSWORD

$include parser.h
$include syscom.h
$include int$keys.h
$include err.h
$include keys.h

   prompt ''
   parser = "!PARSER"
   @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS

   call @parser(PARSER$RESET, 0, @sentence, 0)
   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, token, keyword)   ;* verb

   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, token, keyword)
   if token.type = PARSER$END then
      account = upcase(@logname)
      own = @true
   end else
      account = upcase(token)
      own = (account = upcase(@logname))

* 20 Aug 26 Windows port - A TRAILING TOKEN IS REFUSED, NOT DISCARDED.
*
*   This used to read the account and stop looking, so "MODIFY.PASSWORD DON
*   hunter2" set account = DON, threw "hunter2" away without a word, and
*   prompted for the password as though nothing had happened.  Every visible
*   sign said it worked, because it did work - just not the way the person
*   typing it believed.
*
*   THE COST OF SAYING NOTHING IS THAT THE PASSWORD IS ALREADY SOMEWHERE ELSE
*   by the time the prompt appears: in SD's command stack, and in a shell's
*   history and process list if the verb is ever reached from one.  Refusing
*   does not put it back, but it tells the person to go and deal with it
*   instead of leaving them believing the command line form is supported.
*
*   IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT "ACCEPT IT IF GIVEN".  A password on a command line
*   is the thing this whole credential model exists to avoid - it is why
*   gplbld/verify-scramlogin.ps1 generates its test password and feeds it on
*   stdin, and why SCRAM was written at all.  Making the convenient form work
*   would be undoing that.
*
*   Found 20 Aug 2026 by the owner typing it.  The parser is already positioned
*   after the account, so one more GET.TOKEN is the whole test.
      call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, token, keyword)
      if token.type # PARSER$END then
         stop sysmsg(5276)  ;* A password is never given on the command line...
      end
   end

   * Only an administrator may set an account other than their own.

   if not(own) and not(kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1)) then
      stop sysmsg(2001)  ;* Command requires administrator privileges
   end

   * The account has to be a real one.

   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'accounts' to acc.f else
      stop sysmsg(2200)  ;* Cannot open accounts register
   end

   read acc.rec from acc.f, account else
      close acc.f
      stop sysmsg(5018, account)  ;* Account %1 not in register
   end
   close acc.f

   * Has this account got a credential yet?  The first one cannot require the
   * previous password, since there is not one.  That is the installer's path
   * for setting the initial SDSYS password, and it is why the install has to
   * happen before the machine is exposed to anybody.

*  19 Aug 26 Windows port - CRED$STOREDKEY, where this read CRED$VERIFIER.
*  A VERSION 1 RECORD THEREFORE READS AS "NO CREDENTIAL", which is the
*  intended migration: it cannot be checked by !CRED_VERIFY and cannot be
*  converted without the plaintext, so the first MODIFY.PASSWORD after the
*  upgrade replaces it without demanding the old password.  That is not a way
*  in - reaching this line at all takes either administrator rights or an
*  elevated session as the account itself, and either can already reset the
*  credential outright.
   has.cred = @false
   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'$cred' to cred.f then
      read cred.rec from cred.f, account then
         if cred.rec<CRED$STOREDKEY> # '' then has.cred = @true
      end
      close cred.f
   end else
      stop 'Cannot open the $CRED register.  Has the install completed?'
   end

   if not(has.cred) then
      crt 'Account ' : account : ' has no password set.  Setting the first one.'
   end

   * Changing your own password requires the current one.  An administrator
   * resetting someone else's cannot be expected to know it.

   if own and has.cred then
      display 'Current password: ' :
      input current HIDDEN
      display

      ok = @false
      call !CRED_VERIFY(account, current, ok)
      if not(ok) then
         crt 'Password not changed.'
         @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS
         stop
      end
   end

* 26 Aug 26 Windows port - SAY WHAT ENTER DOES, BEFORE IT IS PRESSED.  Owner,
*   26 Aug 2026: "you need to tell the user what the result of pressing enter
*   rather than entering a password is".
*
*   IT WAS ONLY EVER EXPLAINED IN THE OTHER PROMPT.  LOGIN's require.credential
*   opens with sysmsg(10089), a paragraph that spells the option out; this
*   routine - the one an install actually ends on - went straight from "Setting
*   the first one" to "New password:" and said nothing.  So the way out existed
*   and was invisible at the moment somebody wants it.
*
*   AND IT IS THE FIRST LINE ONLY, WHICH IS WORTH SAYING BECAUSE THE PROMPTS
*   LOOK ALIKE.  The empty test is on pw1.  An empty REPEAT is not a decline -
*   it simply fails to match, costs an attempt and says so.
*
*   TWO CONSEQUENCES, BECAUSE THE SAME KEYSTROKE MEANS DIFFERENT THINGS HERE.
*   With no credential yet, declining leaves the account unreachable over ssh
*   and the API.  Changing an existing one, it just keeps the old password -
*   telling that user about ssh would be alarming and wrong.
   crt
   if has.cred then
      crt 'Press Enter on an empty line to leave the password unchanged.'
   end else
      crt 'Press Enter on an empty line to set no password for now.  The'
      crt 'account then works only at this computer, from a session run as'
      crt 'administrator; ssh and the SD API will refuse it until a password'
      crt 'is set.  SD asks again the first time you open the account.'
   end
* 26 Aug 26 Windows port - THREE ATTEMPTS AT THE PAIR, NOT ONE.  Owner,
*   26 Aug 2026, after mistyping the password at the END OF AN INSTALL: the two
*   entries disagreed, this said "Passwords do not match.  Password not
*   changed." and stopped, and the installer moved on to the check with the
*   account still passwordless.
*
*   THE ONE-SHOT WAS DEFENSIBLE FOR MODIFY.PASSWORD TYPED AT THE PROMPT - the
*   user simply types it again - AND NOT FOR THE PLACE IT IS ACTUALLY REACHED
*   FROM.  finish-install.ps1 runs this once as the last act of an install;
*   there is no prompt to type it at again, and the next chance is the next
*   login.  Same code, two situations, and the harsher one is the one nobody
*   chose to be in.
*
*   AN EMPTY ENTRY STILL LEAVES AT ONCE and is not counted as an attempt: it is
*   how somebody declines, and re-asking would turn a decision into a fight.
   max.tries = 3
   tries = 0

   loop
      tries += 1

      display 'New password: ' :
      input pw1 HIDDEN
      display

      if pw1 = '' then
         crt 'Password not changed.'
         @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS
         stop
      end

      display 'Repeat new password: ' :
      input pw2 HIDDEN
      display

      if pw1 = pw2 then exit

*     THE COUNT IS SAID OUT LOUD so the loop is visibly finite from inside it.
      crt 'Passwords do not match.  That was attempt ' : tries : ' of ' : max.tries : '.'

      if tries >= max.tries then
         crt 'Password not changed.'
         @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS
         stop
      end

      crt
   repeat

   ok = @false
   call !CRED_SET(account, pw1, ok)
   if ok then
      crt 'Password set for account ' : account
      @system.return.code = 0
   end else
      crt 'Unable to set password for ' : account : ', status ' : status()
      @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS
   end

   return
end

* END-CODE
